The '''2023 FA Cup final''' was the final match of the [[2022–23 FA Cup]], the 142nd season of the oldest [[Association football|football]] tournament in the world, the [[FA Cup|Football Association Challenge Cup]]. It was played at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London on 3 June 2023 between [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], making it the first time the [[Manchester derby]] was contested in a cup final.

Manchester City won the match 2–1 for their seventh FA Cup title and second [[Double (association football)|double]]. [[İlkay Gündoğan]] broke the record for the fastest goal ever in an FA Cup final, scoring after just 12 seconds.

As winners Manchester City qualified for the [[2023–24 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]] tournament via their position in the [[2022–23 Premier League]], the [[2023–24 UEFA Europa League]] position went to the sixth-placed Premier League team ([[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]]), while the [[2023–24 UEFA Europa Conference League qualifying phase and play-off round#Play-off round|2023–24 UEFA Europa Conference League play-off round]] spot allocated to the team that finished sixth in the Premier League was awarded to the seventh-placed team ([[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]]).

As winners of both the Premier League and FA Cup, Manchester City qualified for the [[2023 FA Community Shield]], which they lost [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|on penalties]] to Premier League runners-up [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]].

==Background==
The 2023 final marked the 100th anniversary of the first FA Cup final to be played at the original [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|Wembley Stadium]], the venue replaced by the [[Wembley Stadium|current stadium]] in 2007.<ref>{{cite news |title=The 1923 FA Cup final: 100 years of lessons learned and absolutely not learned |url=https://www.football365.com/news/opinion-1923-fa-cup-final-100-years-of-lessons-learned-lessons-not-learned-west-ham-bolton |website=Football365 |date=22 April 2023 |accessdate=24 April 2023 }}</ref> It was Manchester United's 21st appearance in the FA Cup final, tying the record set by Arsenal on their last appearance in [[2020 FA Cup final|2020]], and their first since [[2018 FA Cup final|2018]]. Manchester City made their 12th FA Cup final appearance and their first since [[2019 FA Cup final|2019]].

It was the first time that the two Manchester clubs met in the FA Cup final, but the tenth such meeting at any stage of the competition and the 190th [[Manchester derby]] in total. The two clubs split the results between them in the Premier League during the season; City won the first match 6–3 at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] in October 2022,<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man City 6–3 Man Utd: Erling Haaland and Phil Foden score hat-tricks |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/63024245 |website=BBC Sport |date=2 October 2022 |accessdate=24 April 2023 }}</ref> while United won 2–1 at [[Old Trafford]] in January 2023.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man Utd 2–1 Man City: Marcus Rashford scores winner in Manchester derby |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64195236 |website=BBC Sport |date=14 January 2023 |accessdate=24 April 2023 }}</ref>

Manchester City were seeking to win the [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]] of the Premier League, FA Cup and [[2022–23 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]], attempting to emulate the feat managed by Manchester United in [[1998–99 Manchester United F.C. season|1998–99]]; they faced [[Inter Milan]] in the [[2023 UEFA Champions League final]] a week after the FA Cup final, winning 1–0 and completing the treble. Manchester United were looking to secure their second trophy under manager [[Erik ten Hag]], having won the [[2022–23 EFL Cup|EFL Cup]] in February 2023, aiming for their first ever domestic cup double. They also had once successfully denied an opponent of a continental treble chase before – in [[1977 FA Cup final|1977]] at the expense of [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]].

A UK-wide [[2022–present United Kingdom railway strikes|train strike]] took place on the day of the final.<ref>{{cite news |first=Charley |last=Adams |title=Train strikes hit FA Cup final and Beyoncé concert |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-65797033 |website=BBC News |date=3 June 2024 |accessdate=28 April 2024 }}</ref>

