As Premier League clubs, both Manchester City and Manchester United entered the FA Cup in the third round. City began with a 4–0 home victory over [[2022 FA Cup final|2022 finalists]] [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] at the City of Manchester Stadium,<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=8 January 2023 |title=Manchester City 4–0 Chelsea |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64193420 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> while United beat [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] 3–1 at Old Trafford.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=6 January 2023 |title=Manchester United 3–1 Everton |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64182366 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> In the fourth round, City again played at home, where they beat 14-time FA Cup winners Arsenal 1–0.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=27 January 2023 |title=Manchester City 1–0 Arsenal |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64417476 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> United also faced another home tie in the fourth round, claiming another 3–1 victory over [[Reading F.C.|Reading]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sutcliffe |first=Steve |title=Manchester United 3–1 Reading |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64429931 |date=28 January 2023 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> They managed yet another 3–1 home win in the fifth round, this time against [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Emons |first=Michael |date=1 March 2023 |title=Manchester United 3–1 West Ham |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64799759 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> while City beat [[Bristol City F.C.|Bristol City]] 3–0 at [[Ashton Gate Stadium]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |date=28 February 2023 |title=Bristol City 0–3 Manchester City |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64788956 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Manchester City returned to the City of Manchester Stadium, where they recorded a 6–0 win over [[Burnley F.C.|Burnley]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=18 March 2023 |title=Manchester City 6–0 Burnley |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64984170 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> while United won 3–1 at Old Trafford for the fourth game in a row, Fulham this time the team on the receiving end.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=19 March 2023 |title=Manchester United 3–1 Fulham |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64984201 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> The semi-finals were both played at Wembley Stadium, with City facing [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]] on 22 April and United taking on [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]] the next day. City won their tie 3–0,<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=22 April 2023 |title=Manchester City 3–0 Sheffield United |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/65347141 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref> while United and Brighton played out a goalless draw [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|after extra time]] that had to be decided on penalties; United won the shoot-out 7–6.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=23 April 2023 |title=Brighton 0–0 Man Utd (6–7 on pens) |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/65347158 |access-date=23 April 2023}}</ref>
